    "created_at": "2015-11-12T11:57:46Z",
    "updated_at": "2015-11-12T11:57:46Z",
    "body": "Swapping might be useful for some cases, but for the use case that started this thread - high performance computing where a worker acts as a thread, and where a worker can go to sleep synchronously waiting for a wakeup from another thread - it will not be appropriate.  Here, it is much more important that the program have knowledge of the number of threads available to it, so that it can do its own resource scheduling with that information.  Additionally, the thread will not normally return to its event loop so that it can be swapped out.  Also, being swapped back in means having to restore state, which is going to require coordination with other threads since some of the state is stored there (shared memory objects).\n\nIn that case, some means of detecting that a worker failed to be allocated to an actual independent thread is required.\n\nSo not to shoot down the idea, which could work, but we need something else here.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
